    "case_summary": "An 18-year-old male high school student from South China presented with unremitting fever with peak temperature over 40°C and simultaneous headache for 6 days. One of his classmates was known to have influenza A infection.\n\nPatient had cervical and inguinal lymphadenopathy with maximum diameter of 15mm. No meningeal irritation signs were detected. Later during hospitalization, the patient developed seizures and lost consciousness.\n\n{'Laboratory Tests': ['Antinuclear antibodies were positive at 1:100 with speckled pattern', 'Extractable nuclear antibodies, antiphospholipid antibodies, antineutrophil cytoplasmic antibodies were all negative', 'Bone marrow smear and tissue showed trilineage hematopoiesis with histiocytes and hemophagocytosis', 'Cerebrospinal fluid analysis showed:       - Nucleated cells count: 454 (0-5) ×106/L, with 75% mononuclear cells       - Protein level: 4001 (150-450) mg/L       - Normal glucose and chlorides       - Opening pressure: over 300 mm H2O'], 'Imaging Studies': {'Radiological Findings': 'Both lungs were normal and the spleen was moderately enlarged', 'MRI Findings': {'Image Title': 'Cranial MRI', 'Image Description': 'Leptomeninges thickening and enhancement observed in contrast-enhanced T1 weighted image'}}, 'Pathological Studies': {'Lymph Node Biopsy': {'Histopathology': 'Groups of apoptotic bodies, abundant pink cytoplasmic fragments and small blue dots of fragmented nuclei in confluent necroptosis area', 'Immunohistochemistry': 'Markedly expanded paracortex (CD3+ T cells), limited CD20+ B cells in residual reactive follicles, positive CD68 and myeloperoxidase in histiocytes'}}}",
